Why Is This Brute Force?

The problem is that maxPathFromNode() repeatedly traverses the same subtree.

For example, in a skewed tree:

1
 \
  2
   \
    3
     \
      4

When calculating the answer for 1, we traverse:

2 → 3 → 4

Then when calculating the answer for 2, we traverse:

3 → 4

Then again:

4

So the same nodes are processed repeatedly.

Complexity

Worst case:

Time  = O(n²)
Space = O(h)

Approach 2 — Better: Memoization

The problem in Approach 1 is repeated calculation.

For example:

maxPathFromNode(3)

may be calculated multiple times.

We can store the maximum downward gain for every node.

Use:

Map<TreeNode, Integer>

Then each node's gain is calculated only once.

Code

class Solution {

    Map<TreeNode, Integer> memo = new HashMap<>();

    public int maxPathSum(TreeNode root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        int left = Math.max(getGain(root.left), 0);
        int right = Math.max(getGain(root.right), 0);

        int current = root.val + left + right;

        int leftAnswer = maxPathSum(root.left);
        int rightAnswer = maxPathSum(root.right);

        return Math.max(
            current,
            Math.max(leftAnswer, rightAnswer)
        );
    }

    private int getGain(TreeNode root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (memo.containsKey(root)) {
            return memo.get(root);
        }

        int left = getGain(root.left);
        int right = getGain(root.right);

        int gain = root.val + Math.max(
            0,
            Math.max(left, right)
        );

        memo.put(root, gain);

        return gain;
    }
}

