Every gain is calculated once:

Time = O(n)

But we use a HashMap:

Space = O(n)

plus recursion stack.

This is good, but we can eliminate the HashMap completely.

Approach 3 — Optimal: Postorder DFS + Global Answer

This is the standard optimal solution.

The key insight is:

For every node, calculate the maximum gain that this node can provide to its parent.

A node can provide only one side to its parent.

Why?

Suppose:

        X
       / \
      A   B

If X sends a path to its parent, it can go:

parent → X → A

or:

parent → X → B

It cannot send:

parent → X → A
             ↓
             B

because that would require branching at X.

So the value returned to the parent is:

root.val + max(leftGain, rightGain)

But the answer itself can use both sides:

leftGain + root.val + rightGain

That's the central idea.

Your Code

Your code is the optimal approach:

class Solution {
    int ans = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

    public int maxPathSum(TreeNode root) {
        helper(root);
        return ans;
    }

    int helper(TreeNode root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        int left = Math.max(helper(root.left), 0);
        int right = Math.max(helper(root.right), 0);

        int pathsum = root.val + left + right;

        ans = Math.max(ans, pathsum);

        return root.val + Math.max(left, right);
    }
}

This is O(n) time and O(h) recursion space.

